  "intro_en": "This book teaches Welsh the way Lingua Latina per se Illustrata teaches Latin: by reading one continuous story written entirely in Welsh, from the first page.\n\nRead for meaning, not for every word. Each chapter adds only a little new language, and the story, the pictures and what you already know are meant to make it clear. If a sentence is unclear, read on: the next sentence often answers it.\n\nUse the margin. The notes beside the text are in Welsh only. \"mawr ↔ bach\" means opposites. \"=\" gives a simpler way of saying the same thing. \"gadair ← cadair\" shows the ordinary form of a word whose first letter has changed (a mutation). A small picture shows an object.\n\nRe-read. When a chapter feels easy, read it again with the notes hidden. A second, faster reading is where much of the learning happens. Listen to the audio where it exists.\n\nDo the Pensa, ideally aloud. A practises the chapter's grammar, B its new words, C asks questions about the story. Each starts with a worked example. Nothing is locked behind a score: check, show the answer, or move on.\n\nExport the Anki cards for each chapter you finish (or all chapters so far) and review them daily. The cards show the Welsh word with an example sentence from the story.\n\nThe Welsh is southern Welsh, as taught by the national Learn Welsh courses.\n\nPlease note: all chapters are drafts. They were written with AI assistance and have not yet been checked by a fluent Welsh speaker. Corrections are welcome."
